    },
    {
      "id": "6876199e-62cf-4fb4-87a4-0696f7fa8621",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "d599b086-05b7-4727-a467-50ee8f193c3e",
      "type": "columns",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "ce4c00c5-1415-46b9-9fcd-8c96077687d2",
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "21dd2e74-960b-4dae-a124-bdf3f2d78b7e",
              "type": "paragraph",
              "html": "<strong>AAA</strong> videogames, also known as “<strong>triple-A</strong> games,” refers to the type of video games that are typically developed &amp; published by “major” companies (such as <a href=\"#/revolutionaries-at-sony\">Sony</a>, <a href=\"#/the-history-of-nintendo-1980-1991\">Nintendo</a>, Microsoft, Bethesda, Valve, Rockstar, Activison Blizzard, Ubisoft, EA, Capcom, Square Enix, etc.) with large budgets, high production values, &amp; significant marketing efforts with the aim of creating blockbuster titles with broad pop cultural appeal. The term AAA is used to distinguish a particular segment of the videogame industry from smaller, independent or hobbyist developers &amp; their games, often referred to as <a href=\"#/indie\">indies</a> as well as games by studios &amp; publishers with relatively small budgets, known as AA (double-A) games."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "0910042f-fa64-4225-8744-7c71335809bd",
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
              "id": "930def5e-7efa-4c65-83dd-fad3c15485e0",
              "type": "image",
              "src": "./assets/images/untitled-1a0690dad781.webp",
              "caption": "<strong><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>Horizon: Zero Dawn</em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></strong><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em><em>, developed by Guerrilla Games &amp; published by Sony Interactive Entertainment</em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em></em>",
              "alt": "Untitled"
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "75debc95-9c88-446f-8a9b-3df97d2ee3a8",
      "type": "paragraph",
      "html": "AAA games are typically designed to appeal to a broad range of audiences, often featuring cutting-edge graphics, engaging yet accessible gameplay mechanics, <a href=\"#/open-world\">expansive</a>, detailed worlds, &amp; rich <a href=\"#/multiplayer\">multiplayer</a> modes. Their budget also allows them to include elements often absent in smaller titles such as celebrity voice acting, <a href=\"#/motion-capture\">motion capture</a>, &amp; licensed or orchestral soundtracks. Some AAA games however- some may argue increasingly many- are known to suffer from a lack of creative vision or publisher restraints &amp; complications leading to shallow, rushed releases, uninspired reskins of other popular games, or blatant cash grabs."
    },
    {
      "id": "7678ad6e-d8c2-474f-ad1e-796684bbf5e6",
      "type": "columns",
      "children": [
        {
          "id": "5b089deb-a4ff-4279-a62a-55184adbc510",
          "type": "column",
          "children": [
            {
